1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:13,651
On the fourth of July, 2014, a man in a black robe and a black turban climbed the pulpit of the Great Mosque of al-Nuri, in Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city.

2
00:00:14,751 --> 00:00:25,033
Until that day, almost no one outside his movement knew what he looked like. Now, in front of the cameras, he claimed to be the leader of all Muslims: a caliph.

3
00:00:26,133 --> 00:00:33,771
Over the next few years, more than forty thousand people from around the world travelled to Iraq and Syria to join him.

4
00:00:34,871 --> 00:00:40,673
The United States offered twenty-five million dollars for information leading to him.

5
00:00:41,773 --> 00:00:53,144
Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi turned a beaten insurgent group into a self-declared state that ruled millions of people and inspired murder around the world. This is how.

6
00:00:55,344 --> 00:01:06,395
He was born Ibrahim Awwad Ibrahim al-Badri in 1971, near Samarra, north of Baghdad, into a religious Sunni Muslim family.

7
00:01:07,495 --> 00:01:20,231
People who knew him as a young man described someone quiet and shy, who loved football and Quranic recitation. He studied in Baghdad and went on to earn a doctorate in Quranic studies.

8
00:01:21,331 --> 00:01:31,869
Through the 1990s, under the dictatorship of Saddam Hussein, he lived modestly in Baghdad, studying and leading prayers at a local mosque.

9
00:01:32,969 --> 00:01:46,793
Everything changed in 2003, when a US-led coalition invaded Iraq and overthrew Saddam. Sunni Arabs, who had dominated the old regime, lost power, and an insurgency spread.

10
00:01:48,993 --> 00:02:00,684
He helped found a small militant group. In early 2004 he was detained by American forces and held at Camp Bucca, a huge prison camp in the desert.

11
00:02:01,784 --> 00:02:15,928
Bucca held thousands of men: committed militants, officers from Saddam's disbanded army, and ordinary people swept up in raids. Analysts later called it a meeting place for the future leaders of the Islamic State.

12
00:02:17,028 --> 00:02:31,129
He was released in December 2004, judged a low-level threat. Later he joined the network of al-Qaeda in Iraq, the group founded by the Jordanian militant Abu Musab al-Zarqawi.

13
00:02:32,229 --> 00:02:45,371
Al-Qaeda in Iraq bombed markets, mosques and crowds, deliberately targeting Shia Muslims to provoke a sectarian war. By 2006, Iraq was in exactly that kind of war.

14
00:02:47,571 --> 00:03:01,096
By 2010 the group, now calling itself the Islamic State of Iraq, had been badly beaten by American troops and Sunni tribal fighters. In April, its top two leaders were killed.

15
00:03:02,196 --> 00:03:15,017
In May 2010, the group named a new leader: a little-known scholar who now called himself Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi. Even many of his own fighters had never seen his face.

16
00:03:16,117 --> 00:03:25,077
He rebuilt the group by killing rivals and informers, recruiting former officers of Saddam's army, and breaking members out of prisons.

17
00:03:26,177 --> 00:03:35,713
Two events helped him. American troops left Iraq at the end of 2011. And across the border, Syria collapsed into civil war.

18
00:03:36,813 --> 00:03:54,904
He sent fighters into Syria. In April 2013 he announced that his group now spanned both countries: the Islamic State of Iraq and al-Sham, known in English as ISIS. In early 2014, al-Qaeda's leadership publicly cut ties with him.

19
00:03:57,104 --> 00:04:09,840
In June 2014, ISIS attacked Mosul. Iraqi army units many times larger than the attacking force collapsed and fled, abandoning weapons and vehicles.

20
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:24,892
On the twenty-ninth of June, the group declared a caliphate, a state claiming authority over all Muslims, with al-Baghdadi as its caliph. Muslim scholars and governments almost everywhere rejected the claim.

21
00:04:25,992 --> 00:04:34,440
His sermon at al-Nuri was filmed and posted online. For years, it was the only time most of the world saw him speak.

22
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:44,244
At its height, the self-declared state held cities across Iraq and Syria, from Mosul to Raqqa, and ruled over millions of people.

23
00:04:45,344 --> 00:04:57,589
It ran courts, collected taxes and printed school textbooks. It also staged public executions, punished people for smoking or playing music, and blew up ancient monuments.

24
00:04:59,789 --> 00:05:14,851
On the third of August, 2014, ISIS fighters attacked Sinjar, in northern Iraq, the homeland of the Yazidis, an ancient religious minority that ISIS regarded as unbelievers to be wiped out.

25
00:05:15,951 --> 00:05:27,535
Thousands of Yazidi men and older women were killed. Thousands of women and girls were taken and sold into sexual slavery, and boys were forced to train as fighters.

26
00:05:28,635 --> 00:05:42,032
In 2016, a United Nations commission of inquiry found that ISIS had committed genocide against the Yazidis. Years later, thousands of the abducted were still missing.

27
00:05:43,132 --> 00:05:52,305
Christians, Shia Muslims and other minorities were driven from their homes or killed. Sunni Muslims who resisted were killed too.

28
00:05:53,405 --> 00:06:06,909
In 2018 the United Nations reported more than two hundred mass graves in areas of Iraq that ISIS had held, which it said could contain up to twelve thousand victims.

29
00:06:09,109 --> 00:06:20,331
Al-Baghdadi's group recruited through slick videos and social media. Its films of hostages being murdered were made to shock the world, and to draw in new followers.

30
00:06:21,431 --> 00:06:33,996
According to a 2018 study by King's College London, about forty-one thousand foreigners from some eighty countries joined it, including thousands of women and children.

31
00:06:35,096 --> 00:06:46,637
It also directed or inspired attacks abroad. In November 2015, gunmen and suicide bombers killed one hundred and thirty people in Paris.

32
00:06:47,737 --> 00:06:58,041
Brussels, Nice, Manchester and Sri Lanka followed, among many others. Far more people died in its attacks in Iraq, Syria, Afghanistan and Africa.

33
00:07:00,241 --> 00:07:10,759
From 2014, a coalition led by the United States bombed ISIS, while Iraqi troops, Kurdish fighters and others fought it on the ground.

34
00:07:11,859 --> 00:07:27,859
Mosul was retaken in July 2017, after nine months of fighting that left its old city in ruins. The al-Nuri mosque, where he had preached, had been blown up by ISIS fighters as Iraqi forces closed in.

35
00:07:28,959 --> 00:07:39,028
Raqqa fell that October. In March 2019, at the Syrian village of Baghouz, the self-declared caliphate lost the last of its land.

36
00:07:40,128 --> 00:07:47,189
Al-Baghdadi was still at large. That April, he appeared on video for the first time in nearly five years.

37
00:07:49,389 --> 00:08:01,613
He was hiding in a compound in northwest Syria, close to the Turkish border. On the night of the twenty-sixth of October, 2019, American special forces raided it.

38
00:08:02,713 --> 00:08:15,321
The raid was named after Kayla Mueller, an American aid worker who died in ISIS captivity in 2015. US officials said al-Baghdadi had held her personally.

39
00:08:16,421 --> 00:08:26,235
Cornered in a tunnel, he set off an explosive vest. According to the US military, he killed himself and two young children he had taken with him.

40
00:08:28,435 --> 00:08:34,600
In Sinjar, families are still searching for the missing, and the dead are still being identified.

41
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:51,679
His movement did not die with him. As of 2025, Islamic State affiliates remain active in parts of Africa and in Afghanistan, and thousands of former members and their families are still held in camps in Syria.

42
00:08:52,779 --> 00:09:06,176
Al-Baghdadi promised a caliphate that would last for ever. It held its cities for barely three years. What lasts far longer is the harm it did: the missing, the bereaved, and the towns still being rebuilt.
